@* Generic kanban board. Fans @Items out into one column per status and lets the caller supply the
   card markup, the column header action and the per-status empty state, so the task board
   (/project/{id}) and the ideas board (/ideas) share one shell instead of forking it.

   SortableJS contract (wwwroot/js/kanbanflow.js — breaking any of these kills drag SILENTLY):
     1. A droppable column's card container emits class="board-column-cards" + data-status="{enum
        name}". kanbanflow.js:56 binds Sortable to that class only, so a column with
        IsDragEnabled=false is neither a drop target nor a drag source (put:false + pull:false)
        with zero JS changes.
     2. Each card root emits data-task-id="{id}" — owned by CardTemplate's root, not by the board.
     3. The hosting page owns [JSInvokable] OnCardMoved(int id, string newStatus, int beforeItemId).
        Argument 3 is the next rendered card's stable identity (0 = append), NOT a DOM index.
        Items may be a filtered/partial subset; the host resolves the anchor against persisted order.
        data-status must be the ENUM NAME ("InProgress"), never the display name ("In Progress"):
        the page's Enum.TryParse silently swallows a failed parse and the drop is lost.
     4. CardTemplate's root carries @key="{the item's identity}" (#294). KanbanColumn's card loop
        diffs POSITIONALLY (see the comment there), so without it a re-render after a drag writes
        one card's content into another card's DOM node and the board draws cards in columns they
        are not in — silently, with the database perfectly correct. kanbanflow.js reverting the
        drag's DOM mutation is the other half; a board needs both. *@
@typeparam TItem
@typeparam TStatus where TStatus : struct, Enum

@* `kanban-board-shell` makes this section a CONTAINING BLOCK (position: relative — app.css). Without
   it the board leaks a page-wide horizontal scrollbar onto any page that is not /project/{id}:
   Bootstrap's `.visually-hidden` is `position: absolute !important`, KanbanColumn puts two of them in
   every column heading ("column," / "items"), and an absolutely-positioned box is only clipped by
   ancestors in its CONTAINING-BLOCK chain. With every ancestor static, those spans resolve against
   the initial containing block and sail straight through this section's own `overflow-hidden` — so a
   column sitting at x≈900 drags the VIEWPORT's scroll area out to 932px while body.scrollWidth still
   reads a perfectly innocent 390. /project/{id} never showed it only because `.project-shell` happens
   to be `position: relative` and was quietly catching them (#280). *@
<section class="kanban-board-shell flex-grow-1 d-flex flex-column overflow-hidden" aria-labelledby="kanban-board-heading">
    <div class="border-bottom bg-white px-3 py-2">
        <h2 id="kanban-board-heading" class="h6 mb-1" tabindex="-1">@BoardLabel</h2>
        <p id="kanban-board-guidance" class="small text-muted mb-0" data-testid="kanban-board-guidance">@Guidance</p>
    </div>

    <div class="flex-grow-1 d-flex overflow-auto p-2 gap-2"
         style="min-width: 0;"
         data-testid="kanban-board"
         aria-describedby="kanban-board-guidance">
        @foreach (var status in resolvedStatuses)
        {
            <KanbanColumn TItem="TItem" TStatus="TStatus"
                          @key="status"
                          Status="status"
                          Items="ItemsFor(status)"
                          CardTemplate="CardTemplate"
                          DisplayName="@DisplayNameFor(status)"
                          IsDragEnabled="IsDragEnabledFor(status)"
                          IsTerminal="IsTerminalFor(status)"
                          Collapsed="IsCollapsed(status)"
                          CollapsedChanged="CollapsedChangedFor(status)"
                          InertHint="@InertHint?.Invoke(status)"
                          HeaderActions="ColumnHeaderActions?.Invoke(status)"
                          EmptyTemplate="ColumnEmptyTemplate?.Invoke(status)" />
        }
    </div>
</section>

@code {
    /// <summary>Every item on the board; the board fans them out into one column per status.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public IReadOnlyList<TItem> Items { get; set; } = [];

    /// <summary>Maps an item to the column it belongs in.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public Func<TItem, TStatus> StatusOf { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>Maps an item to its identity. Also the default intra-column order.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public Func<TItem, int> IdOf { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>Renders one card. Its root element owns <c>data-task-id</c> (SortableJS contract 2).</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RenderFragment<TItem> CardTemplate { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>Which columns to render. <c>null</c> renders every value of <typeparamref name="TStatus"/>.</summary>
    [Parameter] public IReadOnlyList<TStatus>? Statuses { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Orders the items inside a column. Defaults to ascending <see cref="IdOf"/>.</summary>
    [Parameter] public Func<IEnumerable<TItem>, IEnumerable<TItem>>? OrderColumnBy { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Column heading text. Defaults to the enum name.</summary>
    [Parameter] public Func<TStatus, string>? DisplayName { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether a column takes part in drag-and-drop. Defaults to <c>true</c> for every column.</summary>
    [Parameter] public Func<TStatus, bool>? IsDragEnabled { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether a column is terminal, and so renders as a collapsible rail. Defaults to <c>false</c>.</summary>
    [Parameter] public Func<TStatus, bool>? IsTerminal { get; set; }

    /// <summary>One-line hint shown inside a column that does not accept drags.</summary>
    [Parameter] public Func<TStatus, string?>? InertHint { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Action rendered in a column's header (e.g. the task board's "+ Task" button).</summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment<TStatus>? ColumnHeaderActions { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Placeholder rendered when a column has no items. Falls back to a quiet muted line.</summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment<TStatus>? ColumnEmptyTemplate { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The board's heading.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string BoardLabel { get; set; } = "Board";

    /// <summary>The guidance line under the heading; the board's <c>aria-describedby</c> target.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Guidance { get; set; }

    // Collapse is a board-level layout concern — collapsing one column changes how the remaining
    // width is shared — so the board owns the state and one re-render settles the whole row.
    // Session-only by design: it is not persisted (see design/ui/interactions.md §4).
    private readonly Dictionary<TStatus, bool> collapsed = [];
    private IReadOnlyList<TStatus> resolvedStatuses = [];

    protected override void OnParametersSet()
    {
        resolvedStatuses = Statuses ?? Enum.GetValues<TStatus>();

        // Seed once per status: terminal columns start collapsed. A status already in the map keeps
        // whatever the user last chose, so a re-render never re-collapses a column they opened.
        foreach (var status in resolvedStatuses)
        {
            if (!collapsed.ContainsKey(status))
            {
                collapsed[status] = IsTerminalFor(status);
            }
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Opens a collapsed column. The board owns the collapse map (collapsing one column re-shares the
    /// row's width, so no single column can own that decision), which left the hosting page unable to
    /// react to its own drops: a card dragged into a collapsed terminal rail simply VANISHED into a
    /// 3rem strip — the rail is a live drop target by design, but a silent one. This is the setter the
    /// map was missing, so the page can reveal where the card actually landed (design/ui/interactions.md §4).
    ///
    /// A no-op unless the column is genuinely collapsed, which makes it safe to call after ANY move:
    /// a board that declares no terminal columns (the task board — it never passes IsTerminal, so
    /// nothing is ever collapsed) can never have a collapsed column to open.
    /// </summary>
    public void Expand(TStatus status)
    {
        if (!IsCollapsed(status))
        {
            return;
        }

        collapsed[status] = false;
        StateHasChanged();
    }

    private IReadOnlyList<TItem> ItemsFor(TStatus status)
    {
        // EqualityComparer, not ==: an unconstrained-ish generic cannot use the equality operator.
        var inColumn = Items.Where(item => EqualityComparer<TStatus>.Default.Equals(StatusOf(item), status));
        return [.. OrderColumnBy is null ? inColumn.OrderBy(IdOf) : OrderColumnBy(inColumn)];
    }

    private string DisplayNameFor(TStatus status) => DisplayName?.Invoke(status) ?? status.ToString() ?? string.Empty;

    private bool IsDragEnabledFor(TStatus status) => IsDragEnabled?.Invoke(status) ?? true;

    private bool IsTerminalFor(TStatus status) => IsTerminal?.Invoke(status) ?? false;

    private bool IsCollapsed(TStatus status) => collapsed.TryGetValue(status, out var value) && value;

    private EventCallback<bool> CollapsedChangedFor(TStatus status) =>
        EventCallback.Factory.Create<bool>(this, value => collapsed[status] = value);
}
